ndividuals through a  hecklers veto  unfortunately the DMCA has authorized a  hecklers veto of political speech I  talked about that briefly you know how  can they get away with limiting  specifically political speech that's  what's in the first time absolutely so  this is what I find so problematic is in  2008 John McCain had a number of  campaign videos up on YouTube and an  individual file to take down requests  with Google and said we'd like you to  take down this content and it infringes  upon our copyright and so Google abided  and took down the campaign videos for  two weeks and then the thrust of the  election cycle and in 2012 was the  nature of the infringing material no  there was no infringement or it was it  was a fraudulent claim and in 2012 with  Mitt Romney's campaign the same thing  happened  and perhaps the most egregious example  of this was in the lead up last year to  the SOPA discussions Mike massive stop  online yes we are Mike Masnick with  Tector did an analysis on SOPA and why  he thought it was a terrible law the  exact thing that the first amendments  designed to protect but yet Google was  given a takedown requests and mastic  found out one day what he searched his  own article that it was no longer in the  top results and he contacted Google and  Google said oh we took it down because  if it take down requests and this is  Google which is known to be perhaps one  of the better operators in this field  but you've created a world where one  individual or a handful of individuals  who don't like content or speech you can  take down that content or speech for  potentially political reasons and  obviously again I support copyright when  you have you know pirated material that  needs to go but enabling the system that  allows for hecklers veto is not the  solution is is it that under DMCA when  you get a takedown order do you have to  comply in good faith or does Google are  they supposed to investigate you have a  very strong incentive to take it down  and then investigate and find out after  the fact so this really is like the  hecklers veto or you're shouting down  whereas at least for a short period of  time and again Michael these are easy  solutions which is again let's we  obviously the piracy issues is a serious  problem but let's deal with this one  particular issue let's say if you file a  false fraudulent takedown request with  absolutely no evidence behind it it's  clearly for political machinations that  you will be fined half a million dollars  or at least you will be stuck using  t-mobile or your carrier for the rest of  your life yeah it would stop it in its  tracks and and and if we're serious  about this you know why would content  supporters be against that you're  actually strengthening the best part of  the DMCA for their perspective well  we're gonna leave it there Derek where's  the best place for people to read your  work absolutely I'm publishing quite  widely the Atlantic and the National  Review are hosting some of my content  okay well thank you very much we've been  talking with Derek Khanna he's formerly  of the Republican Study Committee he  writes widely about the intersection of  public policy and information technology  for Reason TV I'm Nick Gillespie
 

